<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Razia Sultan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1205–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Raziyyat-Ud-Dunya Wa Ud-Din, Raziyya, Raḍiyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Razia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> was Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> from 1236 to 1240, the first female Muslim ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and the only one of Delhi. A daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a>, she ran Delhi while he campaigned at Gwalior in 1231–1232, and by a possibly apocryphal account he then named her his heir. She took the throne after rousing the people of Delhi against her half-brother Ruknuddin Firuz and his mother. Turkic nobles who had expected a figurehead resented her assertiveness and her appointment of non-Turkic officers such as the Abyssinian Yaqut. Deposed in April 1240, she married the rebel Altunia and tried to regain the throne, but was defeated in October and killed soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/razia-sultan.jpg" alt="Razia Sultan" width="330" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equestrian_miniature_painting_of_Razia_Sultana,_circa_18th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
